# **📌 Slide 33 — If-Else-If Ladder**  
fileciteturn0file0

### **What is If-Else-If Ladder?**
Used when you have **many conditions**, but only **one** should run.

### **Syntax (from slide):**
```c
if (condition1)
    statement1;
else if (condition2)
    statement2;
else if (condition3)
    statement3;
...
else
    statement;
```

### **Flow Logic:**
- Starts from top  
- First condition that becomes **true** executes  
- Rest are **skipped**  
- If none match → final `else` executes

### **Use case:**
Grading systems, menu selections, category-based decisions.

---

# **📌 Slide 34 — If-Else-If Ladder Program**  
fileciteturn0file0

### **Program from slide:**
```c
#include<stdio.h>
int main() {
    int marks;
    printf("Enter marks:");
    scanf("%d", &marks);

    if (marks > 75) {
        printf("First class");
    }
    else if (marks > 65) {
        printf("Second class");
    }
    else if (marks > 55) {
        printf("Third class");
    }
    else {
        printf("Fourth class");
    }
    return 0;
}
```

### **Logic:**
- If marks > 75 → First class  
- Else if > 65 → Second  
- Else if > 55 → Third  
- Else → Fourth  

### **Note:**
Conditions run sequentially. Only one block executes.

---

# **📌 Slide 35 — Switch Statement (Introduction)**  
fileciteturn0file0

### **What is a switch?**
A selection control mechanism that compares the **value of a variable** with multiple **constant cases**.

### **Use case:**
- Menu-driven programs  
- Choosing operations  
- Replacing long if-else-if ladders  

### **Key Rules:**
- Only works with constant values  
- Each case ends with `break` to avoid fall-through  
- Without break → execution continues into next case  
- Default case runs when none match  

### **Syntax:**
```c
switch (expression) {
    case value1: statements; break;
    case value2: statements; break;
    ...
    default: statements;
}
```

### **Exam Tip:**
Switch **cannot** compare ranges; only discrete fixed values.

